10
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
Threads is a 1984 apocalyptic anti-war drama television film directed
and produced by Mick Jackson and written by Barry Hines.

11
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
A dramatic account of the potential medical, economic, social, and environmental consequences of a nuclear war in Britain, it follows two families in Sheffield as
a confrontation between the United States and the Soviet Union erupts into war and a general nuclear exchange between NATO and the Warsaw Pact.

12
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
An international co-production between the BBC, Nine Network, and Western-World Television Inc.,
the film was shot on a budget of £400,000 (equivalent to £1,290,611 in 2023).

13
01:24:30,000 --> 01:24:36,000
It was the first of its kind to depict a nuclear winter and has been cited as the film "which comes closest to representing the full
horror of nuclear war and its aftermath, as well as the catastrophic impact that the event would have on human culture".

14
01:31:00,000 --> 01:31:06,000
It has been compared to The War Game (1966) and The Day After (1983).

15
01:37:30,000 --> 01:37:36,000
It was nominated for seven BAFTA Awards in 1985 and won for Best Single
Drama, Best Design, Best Film Cameraman, and Best Film Editor.

16
01:44:00,000 --> 01:44:06,000
The film explores themes of great britain, northern
england, despair, yorkshire, nuclear holocaust.

17
01:50:30,000 --> 01:50:36,000
The tone is emotional and thought_provoking.
